WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:13.230
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to our deep dive, where today you will hear Reza Shakhazemi's spiritual ethos as we let the Imam's own first sermon placed first in the collection sound whole, opening not on law but on the negation of every attribute of God.

2
00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.630
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of sincerity toward him is to divest him of all attributes.

3
00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:23.550
<v Speaker 1>We will explore the strange paradox of why this monumental text begins on negation instead of law,

4
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:32.350
<v Speaker 1>We will listen to the sermon heard whole, and we will follow its single grand arc from the unbounded God down to the creation of a world and back up to the book.

5
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.870
<v Speaker 1>Before we analyze it, we must first hear the text in full.

6
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:44.670
<v Speaker 1>Praise be to God, whose laudation those who speak cannot deliver, whose graces those who count cannot number, and whose rightful do those whose strides cannot render.

7
00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:50.350
<v Speaker 1>He whom no far-reaching aspiration can attain, and whom no diving depth of understanding can fathom.

8
00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:57.230
<v Speaker 1>He whose attribute has no bounding limit, no existing description, no appointed time, and no extended term.

9
00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:04.750
<v Speaker 1>He originated all created things through His power, scattered the winds through His mercy, and steadied the trembling of His earth with mighty mountains.

10
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.710
<v Speaker 1>The foremost of religion is the knowledge of Him.

11
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.430
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of knowing Him is to believe in Him.

12
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.990
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of believing in Him is to affirm His oneness.

13
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.470
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of affirming His oneness is to be sincere toward Him.

14
00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.350
<v Speaker 1>And the perfection of sincerity toward him is to divest him of all attributes.

15
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:32.910
<v Speaker 1>For every attribute bears witness that it is other than the object it qualifies, and every such object bears witness that it is other than the attribute.

16
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:42.110
<v Speaker 1>So whoever ascribes an attribute to God, glorified be he, has joined another to him, and whoever joins another to him has made him two.

17
00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:48.030
<v Speaker 1>And whoever makes him two has divided him into parts, and whoever divides him into parts is ignorant of him.

18
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.350
<v Speaker 1>Whoever points to him has confined him.

19
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:55.870
<v Speaker 1>Whoever confines him has counted him, and whoever asks in what is he, has set him apart.

20
00:01:56.510 --> 00:02:02.030
<v Speaker 1>He is being, but not through any coming to be, existent, but not out of form or nothingness.

21
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:07.390
<v Speaker 1>He is with everything, but not by association, and other than everything, but not by separation.

22
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.270
<v Speaker 1>He acts, but not through movements or instruments.

23
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.870
<v Speaker 1>He sees, even when there was nothing of his creation to be seen.

24
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.990
<v Speaker 1>He is alone, for there was none whose company he might seek or whose absence he might feel.

25
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:30.510
<v Speaker 1>He brought forth creation, and originated it from the beginning, without pondering a plan, without profiting from experience, without setting any motion in train, and without the disquiet of any exertion.

26
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:43.230
<v Speaker 1>He gave each thing its appointed span, harmonized their differences, fixed their natures, and impressed upon them their forms knowing them before he made them, encompassing their bounds and their ends, and aware of what joins them and what parts them.

27
00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:50.190
<v Speaker 1>Then he, glorified be he, cleft the atmospheres, and split the surrounding regions and the layers of air.

28
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:56.350
<v Speaker 1>Into them he made water flow, its waves surging and swelling heaped upon the back of a violent thunderous wind.

29
00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:06.350
<v Speaker 1>He commanded the wind to hold the water back and gave it power over the fury of its currents, setting it against the water's bounds, air beneath it spread wide, water above it pouring in flood.

30
00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:13.630
<v Speaker 1>Then he raised up another wind and made it barren, holding it long in its place, driving hard its course and reaching it out far and wide.

31
00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:24.670
<v Speaker 1>He ordered it to stir the deep waters and to churn the waves of the sea until it beat them as a milkskin is beaten and drove them on in the open air as it had driven them in the void, turning their first back upon their last.

32
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.030
<v Speaker 1>There still upon their moving until the water rose in a heaped mass and lifted up a froth.

33
00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:35.230
<v Speaker 1>This froth he raised into the open and empty air, and made of it the seven heavens.

34
00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:44.350
<v Speaker 1>The lowest a stayed wave held back, the highest a roof upheld, and a vault raised high, with no pillar to prop them and no nail to bind them.

35
00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:54.750
<v Speaker 1>Then he adorned them with the ornament of stars and the light of shooting meteors, and hung within them a shining lamp and a luminous moon, in a turning orbit, a moving vault and a wheeling firmament.

36
00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:59.150
<v Speaker 1>Then he parted the heights of the heavens, and filled them with kinds of his angels.

37
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:08.750
<v Speaker 1>Among them are those who prostrate and do not bow, and those who bow and do not stand, and those who stand in ranks and never break them, and those who glorify him and never weary.

38
00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:15.710
<v Speaker 1>No slumber of the eye overtakes them, no wandering of the mind, no weakness of the body, no forgetful heedlessness.

39
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:24.030
<v Speaker 1>Among them are the trustees of his revelation, and the tongues that carry his word to his messengers, and those who come and go with his decree and his command.

40
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.150
<v Speaker 1>Among them are the guardians of his servants and the keepers of the gates of his gardens.

41
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:41.390
<v Speaker 1>Among them are those whose feet are fixed in the lowest depths of the earth, while their necks pass beyond the highest heaven, whose limbs reach past the ends of the world, and whose shoulders are matched to the pillars of the throne.

42
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:50.750
<v Speaker 1>Their eyes are lowered beneath it, and they have wrapped themselves in their wings beneath it, and between them and all below them are veils of majesty and curtains of might.

43
00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:59.710
<v Speaker 1>They do not picture their Lord in any form, nor ascribe to him the marks of things made, nor confine him within places, nor point to him by any likeness.

44
00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:10.670
<v Speaker 1>Then he, glorified be he, gathered a handful of the earth, of the hard and the soft, the sweet and the salt, and poured water over it until it was cleansed, and kneaded it with moisture until it held.

45
00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.070
<v Speaker 1>From it he shaped a figure with curves and joints, limbs and parts.

46
00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:20.830
<v Speaker 1>He said it till it tried, and hardened it until it rang, for a known term and an appointed end.

47
00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:41.630
<v Speaker 1>Then he breathed into it of his spirit, and arose up a human being, endowed with minds to govern its thought, with limbs to serve it, with instruments to wield, and with a knowing by which to tell the truth from the false, taste from taste, and color from color, of being needed of clashing natures and gather opposites, of heat and cold, of moisture and dryness.

48
00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:52.750
<v Speaker 1>Then God asked of the angels the discharge of the trust he had laid on them and the keeping of the covenant of his command, that they bow down before Adam in submission and lower themselves before his honor.

49
00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.390
<v Speaker 1>So he said, Prostrate yourselves before Adam.

50
00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.950
<v Speaker 1>And they prostrated all save Iblis.

51
00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.910
<v Speaker 1>Pride seized him and wretchedness overcame him.

52
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.990
<v Speaker 1>He gloried in his own making of fire and scorned the creature of clay.

53
00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:11.390
<v Speaker 1>So God granted him respite, that his wrath might be earned in full and the trial completed and the promise fulfilled.

54
00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:15.310
<v Speaker 1>And he said, You are among those reprieved until the day of the appointed time.

55
00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:22.350
<v Speaker 1>Then he settled Adam in a dwelling where his life was made pleasant and his abode secure, and he warned him of iblis and his enmity.

56
00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:27.230
<v Speaker 1>But the enemy deceived him, begrudging him his lasting home and his fellowship with the righteous.

57
00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:34.830
<v Speaker 1>And Adam bartered certainty for doubt and resolve for weakness, and took tread in place of gladness and remorse in place of pride.

58
00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:45.310
<v Speaker 1>Then God spread his repentance before him, taught him the word of his mercy, and promised him return to his garden and sent him down to the abode of trial and the begetting of children.

59
00:06:46.510 --> 00:07:04.110
<v Speaker 1>From among Adam's children God chose prophets and took their pledge upon his revelation and the delivering of his message, at a time when most of his creatures had broken their covenant with him, ignored his right, and set up rivals beside him, and the devils had turned them from the knowledge of him and cut them off from his worship.

60
00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:20.430
<v Speaker 1>So he sent among them his messengers and raised up his prophets one after another, as to call them to fulfill the covenant of their creation, to remind them of his forgotten gift, to argue with them by the delivering of the message, to stir up for them of the buried treasures of the intellect, and to show them the signs of his power.

61
00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:30.670
<v Speaker 1>The sky spread above them, the earth laid beneath them, the means of their living, the deaths that carry them off, the ills that age them, and the events that follow upon them.

62
00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.710
<v Speaker 1>God did not leave his creation without a sent prophet.

63
00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.230
<v Speaker 1>a revealed book, a binding proof, or a clear Rd.

64
00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:42.990
<v Speaker 1>His messengers were never made helpless by their small number or by the throng of those who called them liars.

65
00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:49.550
<v Speaker 1>To each earlier one, the name of the one to come was given, and by each later one, the one before was made known.

66
00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.910
<v Speaker 1>So the generations passed and the ages ran on.

67
00:07:53.550 --> 00:08:04.910
<v Speaker 1>Fathers went and sons came after them until God sent Muhammad, his messenger, to fulfill his promise and complete his prophethood, his pledge already taken from the prophets, his signs well known, his birth noble.

68
00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:16.190
<v Speaker 1>The people of the earth in his day were of scattered creeds, divided desires and warring ways, some likening God to his creation, some twisting his names, some pointing to another than him.

69
00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:22.030
<v Speaker 1>By him God guided them out of error and by his standing among them delivered them from ignorance.

70
00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:30.910
<v Speaker 1>Then God chose for Mohammed the meeting with himself, and was pleased to bring him near, and honored him by taking him away from the abode of this world and the place of trial.

71
00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:38.990
<v Speaker 1>He drew him to himself in honor, and left among you what the prophets left to their communities, for they do not abandon them unattended, without a plain Rd.

72
00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:39.950
<v Speaker 1>and a standing beacon.

73
00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.710
<v Speaker 1>He left the book of your Lord among you.

74
00:08:42.510 --> 00:09:01.070
<v Speaker 1>Setting forth it's lawful and it's forbidden, it's obligatory and it's recommended, it's abrogating and abrogated, it's permitted and it's enjoined, it's particular and it's general, it's lessons and its parables, it's unbound and it's bound, it's clear and it's obscure unfolding what it packed close and clarifying what it left dark.

75
00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:06.990
<v Speaker 1>In it are matters whose knowledge is bound upon the servants and matters whose ignorance is forgiven them.

76
00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:11.790
<v Speaker 1>Things it sets down as obligatory that the practice of the prophet is known to have lifted.

77
00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:15.710
<v Speaker 1>and things the practice makes obligatory that the book has left free to leave.

78
00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:20.670
<v Speaker 1>There are duties fixed to their appointed times that fall away when their time has passed.

79
00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:34.750
<v Speaker 1>It marks off the forbidden acts one from another, the great sins against which it threatens the fire, and the small ones for which it holds out hope of forgiveness, and it names acts acceptable in their lesser measure and left open in their greater, binding in neither.

80
00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:40.750
<v Speaker 2>Well, I mean, the sheer weight of that silence after the text ends is palpable.

81
00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:47.310
<v Speaker 2>We are sitting here looking at a massive, complex piece of literature, and it demands a total shift in how we listen.

82
00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:53.310
<v Speaker 2>I am struck by the compiler making such a deliberate, almost jarring choice to place this first in the collection.

83
00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:53.950
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, I hear you.

84
00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.790
<v Speaker 1>It is entirely jarring for a reader.

85
00:09:55.790 --> 00:10:02.990
<v Speaker 2>Because, you know, this is a text renowned globally as a masterful guide on governance, on the ordering of a society, on ethics and statecraft.

86
00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:09.390
<v Speaker 2>So a reader picks this up expecting rules, laws, or maybe a treatise on how to manage an economy.

87
00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:15.350
<v Speaker 2>Instead, the Imam, peace be upon him, opens with a paradox that essentially paralyzes the tongue.

88
00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:21.790
<v Speaker 2>He is using this incredibly elevated speech to announce that speech itself can deliver absolutely nothing of the creator.

89
00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:26.670
<v Speaker 1>And the architecture of that failure is mapped out so precisely in those first few lines.

90
00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:30.590
<v Speaker 1>It is crucial to understand that it's not just a general statement of human modesty.

91
00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.030
<v Speaker 1>We are not just hearing someone say.

92
00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.390
<v Speaker 2>Like A generic poetic humility.

93
00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:36.110
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

94
00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:41.790
<v Speaker 1>The Imam provides 3 parallel clauses and each one dismantles a specific human faculty.

95
00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:43.630
<v Speaker 1>First, praising is defeated.

96
00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.750
<v Speaker 1>He says, whose laudation those who speak cannot deliver.

97
00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:53.710
<v Speaker 1>The very office of words, the mechanics of language which exist to carry meaning from one mind to another breaks down completely.

98
00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.870
<v Speaker 2>Okay, so language is the first casualty.

99
00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.190
<v Speaker 1>Then, second, reckoning is defeated.

100
00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.230
<v Speaker 1>whose graces those who count cannot number.

101
00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:08.830
<v Speaker 1>So the mathematical mind, the human faculty that measures, categorizes, and divides reality into understandable units, is overwhelmed.

102
00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.310
<v Speaker 1>And finally, requital is defeated.

103
00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.510
<v Speaker 1>whose rightful do those who strive cannot render?

104
00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:16.430
<v Speaker 2>So even physical exertion falls short.

105
00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:22.110
<v Speaker 1>Physical exertion, moral effort, the entirety of a creature's attempt to repay its creator through action.

106
00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:22.830
<v Speaker 1>It all fails.

107
00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:28.110
<v Speaker 2>Those 3 defeats, words, measure, and effort encompass the entirety of what a created being can offer.

108
00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:32.750
<v Speaker 2>The complete map of the creature's poverty is drawn in the very first breath of the text.

109
00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:33.870
<v Speaker 2>But I don't buy that yet.

110
00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:37.110
<v Speaker 2>If no word can reach God, how is the whole sermon not self-defeating?

111
00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:37.550
<v Speaker 1>How do you mean?

112
00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:41.630
<v Speaker 2>Well, we are presented with a massive, incredibly eloquent sermon

113
00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.110
<v Speaker 2>describing God in intricate detail.

114
00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:50.110
<v Speaker 2>If praise fails so utterly, why deliver this expansive oration of praise at all?

115
00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.710
<v Speaker 2>It seems to contradict its own premise in the act of being spoken.

116
00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:58.190
<v Speaker 1>That tension you were pointing out is the exact engine driving the text.

117
00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:02.670
<v Speaker 1>The claim here is a work of rigorous logic, not mere poetic humility.

118
00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:07.150
<v Speaker 1>The speaker is establishing A foundational ontological reality.

119
00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:15.390
<v Speaker 1>Before a person can rule an empire rightly, before they can act justly in a courtroom or even know their own self, they must first stand rightly before the real.

120
00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:15.870
<v Speaker 2>I see.

121
00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:17.550
<v Speaker 2>So it is foundational to everything else.

122
00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:23.310
<v Speaker 1>And standing rightly begins with recognizing the absolute hard limit of the human mind.

123
00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:28.510
<v Speaker 1>The sermon must use words to guide the listener up to the very precipice where words fail.

124
00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:34.270
<v Speaker 1>The speech is a ladder designed to be climbed, and the higher you climb, the less luggage you are allowed to carry.

125
00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:35.550
<v Speaker 2>The less luggage, I like that.

126
00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:36.910
<v Speaker 2>So the words are entirely functional.

127
00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:39.710
<v Speaker 2>They serve only to bring the intellect to the edge of its own limitation.

128
00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.710
<v Speaker 2>Can we unpack this ladder analogy a bit more?

129
00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:48.430
<v Speaker 1>Let's look at the first governing analogy the text presides for this ascent, which is an ascending ladder with strict sequential rungs.

130
00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:52.990
<v Speaker 1>The text states, the foremost of religion is the knowledge of him.

131
00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:55.390
<v Speaker 1>That is the first rung on the ladder.

132
00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.790
<v Speaker 1>But knowledge can be purely abstract.

133
00:12:57.790 --> 00:12:59.790
<v Speaker 1>It can be just a collection of facts in your head.

134
00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:01.950
<v Speaker 1>So we step up to the next rung.

135
00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.990
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of knowing him is to believe in him.

136
00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:10.910
<v Speaker 1>Belief requires the heart's assent, moving beyond mere intellectual data into personal conviction.

137
00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:12.510
<v Speaker 1>Then we step up again.

138
00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:16.270
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of believing in him is to affirm his oneness.

139
00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:18.670
<v Speaker 2>Progression tightens dramatically there.

140
00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:24.910
<v Speaker 2>It moves from a generalized belief in a higher power to a specific, unified, piercing focus.

141
00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.950
<v Speaker 2>You cannot just believe in a fragmented pantheon.

142
00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.310
<v Speaker 2>You must unify the vision.

143
00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:29.950
<v Speaker 1>Correct.

144
00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:32.750
<v Speaker 1>And from affirming oneness, we move to the penultimate run.

145
00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:36.510
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of affirming his oneness is to be sincere toward him.

146
00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.830
<v Speaker 1>Sincerity here is not just earnestness.

147
00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:45.150
<v Speaker 1>It means stripping away the ego, purifying the intention so that the devotion is solely exclusively for the one.

148
00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.670
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I follow the climb so far.

149
00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.190
<v Speaker 1>And this leads to the final, astonishing summit of the ladder.

150
00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:54.750
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of sincerity toward him is to divest him of all attributes.

151
00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.350
<v Speaker 1>The climb does not end in a more vivid picture of God.

152
00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:01.310
<v Speaker 1>It does not end in a more detailed, colorful description.

153
00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:09.150
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of knowing, believing, and being sincere results in the complete and total stripping away of every description.

154
00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:10.350
<v Speaker 2>I have to stop you there.

155
00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:15.630
<v Speaker 2>If we divest him of all attributes, doesn't that strip away his mercy, his power, his life?

156
00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.110
<v Speaker 2>How is that not denying who he is?

157
00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.870
<v Speaker 1>It is a terrifying prospect for the mind, isn't it?

158
00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:21.710
<v Speaker 2>Very much so.

159
00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:29.630
<v Speaker 2>If we follow this rigorous logic to its absolute end, it sounds as though we are forbidden from calling him compassionate or powerful or all-knowing.

160
00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:34.190
<v Speaker 2>We lose the very qualities that make any kind of relationship with the divine possible.

161
00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.670
<v Speaker 2>I mean, a believer relies upon the mercy of God.

162
00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:40.470
<v Speaker 2>To divest him of it feels like, well, theological vandalism.

163
00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:44.910
<v Speaker 1>Let's carefully steel man that fear because it is the most natural human reaction to this text.

164
00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.390
<v Speaker 1>We are terrified of losing the personal God.

165
00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:49.710
<v Speaker 1>We are wired psychologically to put a face on things.

166
00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.830
<v Speaker 1>It is the classic old man with a beard sitting on a cloud problem.

167
00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:54.990
<v Speaker 2>Because we need something to grasp onto.

168
00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:55.630
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

169
00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:56.430
<v Speaker 1>Wait, excuse me.

170
00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:57.630
<v Speaker 1>I mean, that is the core of it.

171
00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.150
<v Speaker 1>Human beings understand power by looking at a powerful king.

172
00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:04.510
<v Speaker 1>We understand mercy by looking at a forgiving parent.

173
00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:11.390
<v Speaker 1>We take these finite, bounded experiences, stretch them out as far as our imagination allows, and paste them onto God.

174
00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:15.870
<v Speaker 1>But the teaching here does not deny that God is merciful or powerful or living.

175
00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:21.550
<v Speaker 1>What is denied is the pictured bounded quality that our mind constructs when we hear those words.

176
00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:24.510
<v Speaker 2>Let's pause on that term for a moment, the bounded quality.

177
00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:29.150
<v Speaker 2>For you listening and trying to grasp this, what exactly constitutes a bounded quality?

178
00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:30.430
<v Speaker 1>Think about human mercy.

179
00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:33.390
<v Speaker 1>When a human feels mercy, it is a finite emotion.

180
00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:37.230
<v Speaker 1>It is a feeling that arises in reaction to a specific situation.

181
00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:39.710
<v Speaker 1>It swells, it peaks, and eventually it fades.

182
00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:41.470
<v Speaker 1>It has a beginning and an end.

183
00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:42.470
<v Speaker 1>It is a bounded thing.

184
00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:43.510
<v Speaker 2>It's reactionary.

185
00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:48.350
<v Speaker 1>Yes, and we picture this human quality and project it onto the infinite creator.

186
00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:52.910
<v Speaker 1>It is that pictured limited boundary that the imam is rejecting.

187
00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:58.590
<v Speaker 1>The text argues that every attribute bears witness that it is other than the object it qualifies.

188
00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:00.510
<v Speaker 1>The adjective is not the noun.

189
00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:05.230
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, whoever ascribes an attribute to God has joined another to him.

190
00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:07.070
<v Speaker 2>The logic is unforgiving.

191
00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:15.870
<v Speaker 2>If God is the subject and you attach an attribute as something distinct from his essence, like applying a coat of paint to a wall, you have created a duality.

192
00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:18.910
<v Speaker 2>You have the essence and then you have the attribute floating alongside it.

193
00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.630
<v Speaker 1>And whoever joins another to him has made him too.

194
00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:24.830
<v Speaker 1>Whoever makes him too has divided him into parts.

195
00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:28.270
<v Speaker 1>And whoever divides him into parts is entirely ignorant of him.

196
00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:29.710
<v Speaker 1>Let's use a modern translation here.

197
00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:31.070
<v Speaker 1>Think about building a car.

198
00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:32.670
<v Speaker 1>A car is composite.

199
00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:35.390
<v Speaker 1>It's made of an engine, wheels, a chassis.

200
00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:38.110
<v Speaker 1>If you take away the wheels, it ceases to function as a car.

201
00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:40.350
<v Speaker 1>The car relies on its parts to exist.

202
00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:46.350
<v Speaker 1>If God is made of mercy plus power plus knowledge, then God depends on these distinct parts to be God.

203
00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:47.870
<v Speaker 1>The parts would precede him.

204
00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.790
<v Speaker 2>Which shatters the concept of an ultimate singular uncaused source.

205
00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:52.670
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

206
00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:56.270
<v Speaker 1>The boundary is false, not the reality.

207
00:16:56.950 --> 00:17:04.190
<v Speaker 1>The mind's limit is rejected so that what remains is a mercy that is nothing other than God himself, entirely unfragmented.

208
00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:10.590
<v Speaker 2>So we are not negating the reality of the mercy, but rather the human scale of the mercy we inherently imagine.

209
00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:13.630
<v Speaker 2>We are chipping away the marble of our own assumptions.

210
00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:15.310
<v Speaker 1>That is the exact mechanism.

211
00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:18.830
<v Speaker 1>The tradition inherited this exact mode of approaching the divine.

212
00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:23.550
<v Speaker 1>The Imam opens his magnificent prayers on precisely this note.

213
00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:31.230
<v Speaker 1>He speaks of the God whom the sight of onlookers fall short of reaching and whom the imaginations of those who would describe him are powerless to render.

214
00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:34.030
<v Speaker 2>So the lineage maintains this rigor.

215
00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:37.550
<v Speaker 1>The entire spiritual lineage speaks with this unified grammar of awe.

216
00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.030
<v Speaker 1>They do not praise by claiming to comprehend.

217
00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:43.550
<v Speaker 1>They praise by consessing the total powerlessness of the human imagination.

218
00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:47.470
<v Speaker 1>The Imam systematically dismantles our spatial and limiting concepts.

219
00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:49.870
<v Speaker 1>He says whoever points to him has confined him.

220
00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:53.390
<v Speaker 2>Confined him because pointing implies a coordinate, it draws a border.

221
00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:59.870
<v Speaker 2>You point to an object that occupies a specific volume of space, distinguishing it from the empty space around it.

222
00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:04.830
<v Speaker 2>To point to the boundless is to immediately fundamentally misunderstand what boundless means.

223
00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:11.870
<v Speaker 1>And whoever confines him has counted him, treated him as just one object among many objects in a series.

224
00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:14.350
<v Speaker 1>Whoever asks, in what is he,

225
00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:17.150
<v Speaker 1>encloses him like putting a letter in an envelope.

226
00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:24.590
<v Speaker 1>Whoever asks upon what is he sets him apart, implying he requires a foundation like a chair to rest upon.

227
00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:31.230
<v Speaker 1>Every single mental construct, every categorical box the human mind has evolved to use is taken apart here.

228
00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:36.670
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay isn't stripping every attribute, just a fancy way of saying God is bare nothingness.

229
00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:37.790
<v Speaker 2>I mean, if we say

230
00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:43.550
<v Speaker 2>He is not this and not that and we cannot point to him and he is not in a place and we must strip away every descriptive word.

231
00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:48.190
<v Speaker 2>At the end of that rigorous process, the mind is staring into a blank void.

232
00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:49.390
<v Speaker 1>It feels like emptiness.

233
00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:52.510
<v Speaker 2>It feels practically indistinguishable from non-existence.

234
00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:57.150
<v Speaker 2>If we remove all categories of being as our brains understand them, aren't we just left with nothing?

235
00:18:57.310 --> 00:19:00.870
<v Speaker 1>That is the great peril of the human mind encountering the infinite.

236
00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:04.990
<v Speaker 1>We confuse the absence of a limit with the absence of reality.

237
00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:12.510
<v Speaker 1>Because our brains only know how to process limits, when the limit is removed, our brain screams, void, nothingness.

238
00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:17.230
<v Speaker 1>But the negation happening here is actually the near side of affirmation.

239
00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:17.950
<v Speaker 2>How so?

240
00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:21.470
<v Speaker 1>Let's look at the second governing analogy embedded deeply in this tradition.

241
00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:26.830
<v Speaker 1>The primary testimony of faith itself, the declaration there is no God but God.

242
00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:30.350
<v Speaker 2>The structure of that testimony is entirely built on a sweeping away.

243
00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:31.230
<v Speaker 2>There is no God.

244
00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:32.510
<v Speaker 1>The negation comes first.

245
00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:33.390
<v Speaker 1>It is aggressive.

246
00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:40.270
<v Speaker 1>It sweeps away every idol the imagination sets up, every false boundary, every lesser concept of power or security you might rely on.

247
00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:41.950
<v Speaker 1>But it does not end in a void.

248
00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.670
<v Speaker 1>The sweeping away is immediately followed by the confessing.

249
00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:45.710
<v Speaker 1>but God.

250
00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:48.750
<v Speaker 2>So the affirmation requires the destruction of the false first.

251
00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:54.430
<v Speaker 1>The sole reality is affirmed only after the space has been ruthlessly cleared of the idols of the mind.

252
00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:58.670
<v Speaker 1>Applied to the attributes, the rhythm is the identical mechanism.

253
00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:00.990
<v Speaker 1>There is no all compassionate but God.

254
00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:04.030
<v Speaker 1>The merely pictured bounded mercy is denied.

255
00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:08.430
<v Speaker 1>So the infinite ontological reality of mercy can finally be affirmed.

256
00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:11.070
<v Speaker 2>So the negation is a preparatory act.

257
00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:14.430
<v Speaker 2>It clears the false so the true can have room to be acknowledged.

258
00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:23.870
<v Speaker 2>An Ismaili philosopher from the tradition articulated this brilliantly, using a double negation to prevent the mind from falling into the trap of thinking God is a void.

259
00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:26.350
<v Speaker 2>He taught that the tongue must negate twice.

260
00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:29.310
<v Speaker 2>God is not a thing, and he is not a thing.

261
00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:31.550
<v Speaker 1>Let's unpack that logic because it is vital.

262
00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:34.350
<v Speaker 1>Not a thing clears away the crude physical idol.

263
00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:39.070
<v Speaker 1>It destroys the idea that God is an object in the universe, a being among beings.

264
00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:42.310
<v Speaker 2>But the second part, not a thing, is the ultimate safeguard.

265
00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.390
<v Speaker 2>It stops the mind from turning him into non-existent.

266
00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:49.630
<v Speaker 2>Because even the concept of non-existence or nothingness is still a human category.

267
00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:52.430
<v Speaker 1>It is a concept we invented to describe the physical world.

268
00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:56.430
<v Speaker 2>It is a yardstick we use to measure reality within the physical universe.

269
00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:01.470
<v Speaker 2>We evolved to recognize when a predator is in the brush and when there is no predator in the brush.

270
00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:05.470
<v Speaker 2>Existence and non-existence are binaries we apply to creation.

271
00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:09.870
<v Speaker 2>To apply that binary to the creator of the binary is a massive category error.

272
00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:12.510
<v Speaker 1>The first negation clears the physical idol.

273
00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:17.710
<v Speaker 1>The second negation clears the subtle, intellectual idol hiding inside the mind's own denial.

274
00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:21.870
<v Speaker 1>Nothing of the creature's categories is left clinging to the real.

275
00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:28.750
<v Speaker 1>An early Sufi master compressed this entire theological rigor into a single, devastating definition.

276
00:21:29.230 --> 00:21:33.550
<v Speaker 1>He said that divine oneness is to set the eternal apart from all that comes to be in time.

277
00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:34.830
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I concede the point.

278
00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:37.150
<v Speaker 2>The necessity of it is undeniable now.

279
00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:43.630
<v Speaker 2>If you do not perform this rigorous negation, you inevitably drag the eternal down into the timeline of created, decaying things.

280
00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:46.830
<v Speaker 2>You end up worshipping a highly exalted creature, not the creator.

281
00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:49.950
<v Speaker 2>You worship an idol made of thoughts rather than an idol made of wood.

282
00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:52.230
<v Speaker 2>The grammar of the text is working entirely

283
00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:53.470
<v Speaker 2>to prevent that collapse.

284
00:21:53.590 --> 00:22:00.910
<v Speaker 1>Let's examine that grammar directly in the text, because it governs everything the Imam says as he transitions from the abstract essence down into the act of creation.

285
00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:05.630
<v Speaker 1>The text says, he is being, but not through any coming to be.

286
00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:08.670
<v Speaker 1>existent, but not at a former nothingness.

287
00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:12.750
<v Speaker 1>He affirms the reality, and in the exact same breath, he strips away the limit.

288
00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:15.550
<v Speaker 2>It is a deliberate, rhythmic oscillation.

289
00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.590
<v Speaker 2>He's with everything, but not by association.

290
00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:22.150
<v Speaker 2>You affirm presence, but you immediately deny physical proximity.

291
00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:24.550
<v Speaker 1>And other than everything, but not by separation.

292
00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:28.110
<v Speaker 2>You affirm transcendence, but you deny spatial distance.

293
00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:31.230
<v Speaker 2>He acts, but not through movements or instruments.

294
00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:35.630
<v Speaker 2>Every single positive claim is immediately rescued from becoming a bounded idol.

295
00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:40.910
<v Speaker 1>This specific grammar affirm, then strip the limit, prepares us for the pivotal moment in the text.

296
00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:44.590
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of sincerity toward him is to divest him of all attributes.

297
00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:53.150
<v Speaker 1>And because he is divested of all attributes, because he is bound by nothing, needs nothing, and undergoes no change whatsoever, his act of creation is entirely unique.

298
00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:57.390
<v Speaker 2>If God acts without movement, without instruments, and without any coming to be,

299
00:22:57.790 --> 00:23:00.110
<v Speaker 2>then creation cannot be an act of necessity.

300
00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:05.550
<v Speaker 2>It cannot be God changing his state from non-creator to creator in order to fulfill a need.

301
00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:08.750
<v Speaker 1>This is a profound departure from ancient mythologies.

302
00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:18.190
<v Speaker 1>In many ancient traditions, the gods create humans because they need labor, or they need sacrifices to feed them, or they create out of a sense of cosmic loneliness.

303
00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:23.470
<v Speaker 1>But the unbounded giver creates not to repair some lack within himself.

304
00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:29.950
<v Speaker 2>The text explicitly states, he is alone, for there was none whose company he might seek or whose absence he might feel.

305
00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:33.870
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, the world that follows from him is a sheer unnecessary gift.

306
00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:39.870
<v Speaker 2>The negation we struggled with at the opening is actually the only mechanism that makes the generosity of creation intelligible.

307
00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:44.110
<v Speaker 2>Only a God who is bound by nothing and needs nothing could give being so freely.

308
00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:47.710
<v Speaker 2>The world is not a symptom of divine necessity or cosmic loneliness.

309
00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:48.550
<v Speaker 2>It is an outpour

310
00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:50.510
<v Speaker 2>provoked absolute generosity.

311
00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:52.510
<v Speaker 1>And the text shifts dramatically here.

312
00:23:52.910 --> 00:23:59.870
<v Speaker 1>Having emptied the listener's mind of every finite concept of God, it descends forcefully into the physical drama of cosmology.

313
00:24:00.230 --> 00:24:04.110
<v Speaker 1>The language moves from silent abstraction to violent thundering motion.

314
00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:10.190
<v Speaker 1>The imam describes God cleaving the atmospheres, splitting the surrounding regions in the layers of air.

315
00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.870
<v Speaker 2>The imagery is overwhelmingly forceful, almost terrifying.

316
00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:21.710
<v Speaker 2>Into them he made water flow, its waves surging and swelling, heaped upon the back of a violent, thunderous wind.

317
00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:26.830
<v Speaker 2>We are seeing the raw materials of existence in a state of absolute chaos and turbulence.

318
00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:29.270
<v Speaker 2>It is not a gentle painting of a landscape.

319
00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.710
<v Speaker 2>It is a collision of cosmic forces.

320
00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:36.990
<v Speaker 1>God commands a first wind to hold the water back, giving it power over the fury of the currents.

321
00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:40.430
<v Speaker 1>And then we arrive at the third governing analogy of our deep dive.

322
00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:47.710
<v Speaker 1>The imam says God raised up another wind, a barren wind, and ordered it to churn the ways of the sea until it beat them as a milk skin is beaten.

323
00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:50.750
<v Speaker 2>Let's translate that milk skin analogy for a modern listener.

324
00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:54.750
<v Speaker 2>imamate is deeply grounded in the desert reality of the time.

325
00:24:55.390 --> 00:24:59.550
<v Speaker 2>To make butter, you place milk in an animal skin and you agitate it violently.

326
00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:07.550
<v Speaker 2>You subject A homogeneous liquid to extreme kinetic energy, throwing it back and forth, turning it still into moving its first back upon its last.

327
00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:09.310
<v Speaker 1>It is purposeful agitation.

328
00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:10.710
<v Speaker 2>The turbulence is not random.

329
00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:12.830
<v Speaker 2>It has a highly specific purpose.

330
00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:16.510
<v Speaker 2>It is an extraction of order and solidity from a churning fluid.

331
00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:18.590
<v Speaker 2>It forces a phase change.

332
00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:24.190
<v Speaker 1>The chaos of the primordial waters is beaten like that milk skin until it throws up a froth.

333
00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:28.510
<v Speaker 1>And from that very foam of chaos, he lifts the seven heavens.

334
00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:34.030
<v Speaker 1>The lowest becomes a staid wave held back, the highest a roof upheld and a vault raised high.

335
00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:36.470
<v Speaker 1>Notice the trajectory of this creation account.

336
00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:38.030
<v Speaker 2>It goes from chaos to order.

337
00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:47.550
<v Speaker 1>It begins with violent, thunderous wind and surging flooding waves, and it ends with the intricate adornment of stars, a shining lamp, and a luminous moon in a turning orbit.

338
00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:52.430
<v Speaker 2>The narrative moves from maximum turbulence to absolute mathematical serenity.

339
00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:57.790
<v Speaker 2>The sheer violence of the churning sea resolves into the quiet precision of the wheeling firmament.

340
00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:04.750
<v Speaker 2>Creation is revealed not as a gentle act of decoration, but as the total mastery over immense tumult.

341
00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:10.750
<v Speaker 1>And that mastery is absolute, precisely because the one ordering it is bound by nothing within it.

342
00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:18.590
<v Speaker 1>Then, having constructed this vast, serene vault, the text parts the heights of the heavens and fills them with the ranks of angels.

343
00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:24.590
<v Speaker 1>The Imam lingers on these celestial orders, creating a profound hierarchy of devotion.

344
00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:32.670
<v Speaker 2>The text details angels who prostrate and never bow, those who bow and never stand, those who stand in ranks and never break them, they glorify endlessly.

345
00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:37.230
<v Speaker 2>No slumber of the eye, no wandering mind, no physical weariness ever overtakes them.

346
00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:39.950
<v Speaker 2>Their devotion is perpetual and flawless.

347
00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:47.790
<v Speaker 1>There are trustees of revelation, guardians of servants, keepers of gardens, and at the summit of this hierarchy are beings of an unimaginable scale.

348
00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:49.870
<v Speaker 1>Their feet are fixed in the lowest depths of the earth.

349
00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:51.790
<v Speaker 1>Their necks pass beyond the highest heaven.

350
00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:54.990
<v Speaker 1>Between them and all below are veils of majesty and curtains of might.

351
00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:58.190
<v Speaker 2>It is a staggering image of celestial power.

352
00:26:58.270 --> 00:27:08.230
<v Speaker 1>Yet for all this cosmic grandeur, for all their proximity to the divine, the decisive theological point the Imam makes about these highest angels brings us right back to the beginning of the sermon.

353
00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:10.390
<v Speaker 2>The text says, they do not picture

354
00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:18.990
<v Speaker 2>their lord in any form, nor ascribe to him the marks of things made, nor confine him within places, nor point to him by any likeness.

355
00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:24.350
<v Speaker 1>The very creatures standing nearest the throne engage in an apophatic worship.

356
00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:27.630
<v Speaker 1>Their highest adoration is a negative one.

357
00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:35.150
<v Speaker 1>Even with their immense cosmic knowledge, even with their staggering intellects, they do not attempt to draw a boundary around the boundless.

358
00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:42.110
<v Speaker 1>Negation becomes the highest form of worship, not just for the limited human mind, but for the very apex of creation.

359
00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:44.270
<v Speaker 2>Heaven itself is built on this negation.

360
00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:52.750
<v Speaker 2>Their nearness to God is not measured by having a more vivid, high-definition image of him, but by how completely they have surrendered the need to picture him at all.

361
00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:57.630
<v Speaker 2>They practice the exact divesting of attributes that the opening ladder demanded of us.

362
00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:00.670
<v Speaker 2>The smarter the being, the less they try to put God in a box.

363
00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:05.310
<v Speaker 1>From the unimaginable heights of those angels, the narrative zeros in on a handful of dust.

364
00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:10.110
<v Speaker 1>God gathers earth of the hard and the soft, the sweet and the salt.

365
00:28:10.430 --> 00:28:17.550
<v Speaker 1>He pours water over it, kneads it with moisture, shapes a figure with curves and joints, and lets it dry until it rings like fired clay.

366
00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.150
<v Speaker 2>The contrast is jarring.

367
00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:24.670
<v Speaker 2>From beings whose necks pass the highest heavens, constructed of pure light and obedience, we move to mud.

368
00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:31.790
<v Speaker 2>Clay kneaded of clashing natures, heat and cold, moisture and dryness, a fragile composite inherently unstable thing.

369
00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:34.030
<v Speaker 1>But then he breathed into it of his spirit.

370
00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:42.590
<v Speaker 1>This clay rises up a human being, endowed with minds to govern its thought, limbs to serve it, and a knowing by which to tell the true from the false.

371
00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:49.550
<v Speaker 1>The breath of the spirit awakens the clay to discernment, and immediately this newly quickened creature becomes the center of a cosmic test.

372
00:28:50.030 --> 00:28:53.470
<v Speaker 1>God commands the tireless, flawless angels to bow down before Adam.

373
00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:55.790
<v Speaker 2>And they all prostrate, except Iblis.

374
00:28:55.950 --> 00:29:01.470
<v Speaker 2>The tension we explored earlier regarding idolatry and the limits of the mind manifests here as a massive moral crisis.

375
00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:04.350
<v Speaker 2>Iblis refuses and the text says pride seized him.

376
00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:08.110
<v Speaker 2>He gloried in his own making of fire and scorned the creature of clay.

377
00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:11.310
<v Speaker 1>Iblis exalted his own substance over the command of God.

378
00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:19.310
<v Speaker 1>He looked at the material fire versus clay and made a judgment based entirely on boundaries, forms, and atomic weight, if you will.

379
00:29:19.550 --> 00:29:20.990
<v Speaker 1>He was the first materialist.

380
00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:22.430
<v Speaker 2>He only saw the ingredients.

381
00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:24.430
<v Speaker 1>He failed to see the breath of the spirit.

382
00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:31.030
<v Speaker 1>He practiced a hidden idolatry, worshiping his own origin rather than submitting to the boundless authority that gave the command.

383
00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:36.190
<v Speaker 2>His ego became his idol, so God grants him a respite that the trial might be completed.

384
00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:40.990
<v Speaker 2>Adam is settled in a secure dwelling, warned of the enemy, but the deception occurs.

385
00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:45.150
<v Speaker 2>The text says Adam bartered certainty for doubt and resolve for weakness.

386
00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:48.830
<v Speaker 2>He took dread in place of gladness and remorse in place of pride.

387
00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:52.590
<v Speaker 1>It is the tragedy of the fall, beautifully and painfully compressed.

388
00:29:53.070 --> 00:30:00.350
<v Speaker 1>But God spreads his repentance before him, teaches him the word of mercy, and sends him down to the abode of trial and the begetting of children.

389
00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:05.710
<v Speaker 1>The human story, with all his messiness and clashing natures, truly begins in this descent.

390
00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:09.950
<v Speaker 1>And from among Adam's children, the final movement of the text unfolds.

391
00:30:10.510 --> 00:30:11.550
<v Speaker 1>succession of the prophets.

392
00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:15.550
<v Speaker 2>The imam describes a world that has largely forgotten its origin.

393
00:30:15.950 --> 00:30:21.550
<v Speaker 2>Most of the creatures had broken their covenants, set up rivals, and the devils had turned them from the knowledge of God.

394
00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:27.870
<v Speaker 2>The idols of the mind that we discussed at the very beginning had manifested as literal idols in the physical world.

395
00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:29.630
<v Speaker 1>So God chooses messengers.

396
00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:31.550
<v Speaker 1>He sends them one after another.

397
00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:34.350
<v Speaker 1>Their mission is defined precisely.

398
00:30:34.910 --> 00:30:41.150
<v Speaker 1>to call humanity, to fulfill the covenant, to remind them of the forgotten gift, and to argue with them by delivering the message.

399
00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:47.470
<v Speaker 1>Most importantly, the text says they are sent to stir up for them the buried treasures of the intellect.

400
00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:51.070
<v Speaker 2>The buried treasures of the intellect, that phrasing is so vital.

401
00:30:51.230 --> 00:30:54.110
<v Speaker 2>The intellect here isn't just an organic calculator in the skull.

402
00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:56.830
<v Speaker 2>It is an intuitive organ for recognizing truth.

403
00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:59.630
<v Speaker 1>It is a faculty meant to climb the ladder we discussed.

404
00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:04.790
<v Speaker 2>The capacity to recognize the unbounded God, to climb that initial ladder, is already

405
00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:11.310
<v Speaker 2>within humanity, but it is buried under the debris of clashing natures, earthly desires, and false imaginings.

406
00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:14.350
<v Speaker 2>The prophets do not bring an alien foreign data set.

407
00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:16.910
<v Speaker 2>They excavate what the soul already knows.

408
00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:24.110
<v Speaker 1>They show the signs of his power, the sky spread above, the earthly beneath, the means of living, the inevitability of death.

409
00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.310
<v Speaker 1>And there is a single, unbroken chain of succession.

410
00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:33.390
<v Speaker 1>Each earlier prophet is given the name of the one to come, and each later one confirms the one before.

411
00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:41.070
<v Speaker 1>The generations pass, ages run on, until God sends the Prophet peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

412
00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:45.310
<v Speaker 2>The arrival of the Prophet is the culmination of this immense historical chain.

413
00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:54.750
<v Speaker 2>He is sent to fulfill the promise, his pledge already taken, his signs well known, and he arrives in a world fractured by scattered creeds and divided desires.

414
00:31:54.830 --> 00:31:57.950
<v Speaker 1>The Imam describes the exact nature of those warring ways.

415
00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:02.590
<v Speaker 1>Some likening God to his creation, some twisting his name, some pointing to another than him.

416
00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:08.670
<v Speaker 1>Notice how all these errors are variations of the very idolatry the first sentences of the sermon sought to destroy.

417
00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:10.190
<v Speaker 2>They are failures of negation.

418
00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:12.190
<v Speaker 1>They are failures to divest the attributes.

419
00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:15.950
<v Speaker 1>By the prophet, God guided them out of error and delivered them from ignorance.

420
00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:20.990
<v Speaker 2>And then, having completed his immense mission, God chose for him the meeting with himself.

421
00:32:21.310 --> 00:32:23.950
<v Speaker 2>He drew him away from the place of trial and honor.

422
00:32:25.230 --> 00:32:27.710
<v Speaker 2>But a prophet cannot leave a community untended.

423
00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:31.950
<v Speaker 2>The arc of the narrative must land somewhere concrete for the people left behind.

424
00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:33.150
<v Speaker 1>And it lands on the book.

425
00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:37.950
<v Speaker 1>The text shifts one final time, detailing what the prophet left among the people.

426
00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:46.750
<v Speaker 1>The book of your Lord, setting forth it's lawful and it's forbidden, it's obligatory and it's recommended, it's particular and it's general, it's unbound and it's bound.

427
00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:55.870
<v Speaker 1>The sermon that began in the dizzying heights of an incomprehensible essence now grounds itself in the granular daily details of a legal and moral code.

428
00:32:56.190 --> 00:33:00.030
<v Speaker 2>The structure of the book as described by the imam is intricate and highly demanding.

429
00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:04.990
<v Speaker 2>It marks off forbidden acts, threatens the fire for great sins, holds out hope for small ones.

430
00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:08.350
<v Speaker 2>It names acts acceptable in lesser measure and left open in greater.

431
00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:11.870
<v Speaker 2>It is a text that binds and frees, providing a plain Rd.

432
00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:12.990
<v Speaker 2>and a standing beacon.

433
00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:15.390
<v Speaker 1>This ending is deeply intentional.

434
00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:24.270
<v Speaker 1>The boundless God pours out a world, places an intellect within it, guides it through the long labor of prophets across time, and leaves it with a book.

435
00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:26.670
<v Speaker 1>The written word is left in human hands.

436
00:33:27.150 --> 00:33:31.110
<v Speaker 1>And in his own farewell, the prophet named 2 weighty things left behind.

437
00:33:31.630 --> 00:33:33.470
<v Speaker 1>The book of God and his family.

438
00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:36.590
<v Speaker 1>The living guardianship in the written text unified.

439
00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:42.430
<v Speaker 1>The voice delivering this very sermon is the voice of that guardianship, unpacking the depths of the book.

440
00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:43.870
<v Speaker 2>The ark is perfectly complete.

441
00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:46.270
<v Speaker 2>The sermon is 1 vast, unbroken motion.

442
00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:55.470
<v Speaker 2>The knowledge of God that was declared the foremost of religion at the very beginning is the exact same knowledge that the prophets labored to restore, and it is the exact same knowledge codified in the book.

443
00:33:55.790 --> 00:33:59.070
<v Speaker 2>The end of the sermon loops flawlessly back to its first sentence.

444
00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:02.750
<v Speaker 1>The negation and the creation are not two distinct subjects.

445
00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:04.430
<v Speaker 1>They are one unified teaching.

446
00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:13.150
<v Speaker 1>Before a ruler issues a decree, before justice can be enacted in the world, the soul must let go of the idols the mind naturally manufactures.

447
00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:23.630
<v Speaker 1>A ruler who serves a bounded picture of God, a God he thinks he can comprehend and contain, will inevitably serve an idol of his own power, his own certainty, or his own tribe.

448
00:34:24.350 --> 00:34:27.950
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of sincerity toward him is to divest him of all attributes.

449
00:34:28.430 --> 00:34:33.630
<v Speaker 1>The journey ends not in grasping the divine, but in allowing oneself to be unmade by the boundless real.

450
00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:38.750
<v Speaker 1>Which idol of the mind, which small bounded picture of the real, does your own worship still serve this week?
